  "tldr": [
    "**The model is saturated, and that is the headline.** At the fixed point (`t_high` = 0.039) it catches 99.2% of 3,511 held-out real scams (28 missed) while flagging 6.4% of real English ham (n=345). Every robustness probe therefore has almost no recall left to lose - the failure mode is false positives, not missed attacks.",
    "**Temperature scaling works on the strata we can measure**, but the safe side stays miscalibrated: val ECE 0.0298 -> 0.0053 (reweighted), while `public_ham_english` is still the worst stratum. T = 0.461 (the raw-val fit would be 0.712).",
    "**The abstention band does not exist for this model.** At the 0.95/0.95 targets `t_high` = `t_low` = 0.039 with 0 validation rows between them; no target from 0.80 to 0.99 produces a non-empty band, because the scorer's probabilities are bimodal. Coverage is 100% by construction.",
    "**The human-`suspicious` holdout gets no abstention at all**: 781 of 783 rows (0.0% abstained) are pushed to a definitive verdict. These rows are ambiguous by construction, so `scam` is not automatically wrong - but a three-way system that never uses its third verdict is a two-way system with extra UI.",
    "**Mechanical attacks barely move recall**: the worst probe is spaces_inside_words on `real_scams` (n=500), recall 0.991 vs a clean baseline near 1.000 (-0.001). This is the saturation effect again: a classifier that says `scam` to almost everything cannot be talked out of it.",
    "**The one number that would worry a reviewer is the fresh safe-chat FPR: 5.5% (11/200)** on legitimate messages that share no template with training (max similarity to train 0.61). The Hinglish transfer probe, by contrast, barely changes recall (0.990 -> 0.987 on n=300)."
  ],
